TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "initiation profile similarity",
    "pre-absence",
    "play type reallocation",
    "teammate gain",
    "absence spell",
    "offensive reorganization"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define a teammate's initiation profile similarity to the absent player?",
    "How to identify the absent player's primary play types?",
    "How to measure gain in those primary types during the absence?",
    "What minimum pre-absence and absence games are required for stable estimates?",
    "How to control for positional and minutes-opportunity confounders?",
    "What sample size and power can be expected from the available data?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior work was retrieved; the project has not previously studied the relationship between teammate profile similarity and play-type gain during absences."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Any prior effect sizes, valid metrics, or confounding structures for this claim.",
    "Sample size requirements or feasibility of a test with the available data.",
    "Whether teammate profile similarity is a meaningful predictor of post-absence reallocation."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The claim can be interpreted as: for each absence spell, the teammate whose pre-absence initiation profile is most similar to the absent player's should show the largest increase in initiations in the play types the absent player used most frequently.",
    "The question allows alternative proximity measures (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes), but these confound profile similarity with general opportunity; they must be tested separately or controlled.",
    "With 148 spells and 11 case players, the within-spell rank-correlation approach may be feasible if spells satisfy minimum observational windows; the 11 players suggest effective sample size is limited and standard errors must be clustered.",
    "Measurement of initiation profiles requires a stable pre-absence window of at least 10–15 games; if many spells have shorter windows, the qualifying sample will shrink dramatically.",
    "Confounding by coaching strategy, simultaneous injuries, and defensive adjustments is severe; a falsification strategy is essential.",
    "The outcome 'largest gainer in the absent player's primary types' must be defined carefully to avoid floor effects, regression to the mean, and zero-inflation.",
    "Multiple possible similarity metrics and gain definitions create a multiplicity problem that requires pre-registration and correction.",
    "A test is possible in principle but requires substantial operationalisation before analysis."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Distribution of absence spell lengths and the number of pre-absence games per spell; the description gives 148 spells but not the filter or definitions used.",
    "Number of teammates per spell who meet minimum playing-time and initiation thresholds.",
    "Whether the 11 cases represent 11 distinct players and whether the spells are independent within player.",
    "Accuracy of the inferrer play-type classifier in assigning initiators, especially for low-frequency play types.",
    "Stability of initiation profiles across seasons and under varying defensive schemes."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved; the decision is based entirely on reasoning about the data, question, and methodological risks."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.4,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "After imposing minimum pre-absence and absence game thresholds, the number of qualifying spells may fall well below 148. With only 11 case players, the effective sample for clustered inference is small."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The absent player's primary play types may appear rarely in teammates' pre-absence profiles, making gain estimates noisy and similarity poorly defined."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Initiation profiles rely on the inferrer whose classification errors introduce noise into both similarity calculations and gain measurement."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Post-absence role changes are driven by coaching decisions, positional needs, and defensive schemes, not solely by inherent profile similarity. Similarity may correlate with same-position status, a strong confounder."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier study family was retrieved that directly investigated whether the teammate with the closest initiation profile gains the most in the absent player's primary play types."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Qualifying spells may be too few after applying necessary minimum-game filters, and within-player clustering reduces effective degrees of freedom."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Low-frequency play types lead to zero-inflated gain distributions and unreliable similarity estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The inferrer is not a gold-standard play-type classifier; misclassification can bias both similarity and gain."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Positional replacement, coaching scheme changes, and other simultaneous injuries are unobserved confounders."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "The question allows multiple similarity metrics and gain definitions; testing all without correction inflates false-positive risk."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "If spells are selected based on extreme pre-absence profiles, regression to the mean could produce spurious gain patterns."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "In many spells the 'closest' teammate may be the same-position player, making the similarity metric redundant; the base rate of top-gain by position alone may be high."
    },
    {
      "code": "IMPLEMENTATION_BUG",
      "text": "Complex data pipeline from play-by-play to initiation profiles to similarity and gain; programming errors are a material risk."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.3,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The within-spell rank correlation between pre-absence initiation-profile similarity (absent player vs. teammate) and the teammate's gain in the absent player's primary play types during the absence.",
    "unit": "One absence spell (consecutive missed games by a player) that meets all inclusion criteria.",
    "sample": "All absence spells from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons in which: (a) the absent player played in at least 12 team games in the 30 days before the first missed game; (b) the spell contains at least 5 absence games; (c) at least 5 teammates played in both the pre-absence and absence windows and averaged at least 1.0 initiation per game in the pre-absence window. Exclude spells where the player returned mid-game; exclude garbage-time possessions (last 4 minutes with point differential >= 20).",
    "power": "Expected ~100 qualifying spells with an average of 6 teammates per spell. A one-sample z-test of the proportion of spells where the top-similarity teammate is the top gainer can detect an increase from a chance rate of ~0.17 to 0.30 with power 0.80 at alpha 0.05. Alternatively, Stouffer's combination of spell-level Spearman correlations will be used to increase sensitivity.",
    "controls": "Primary similarity metric: cosine similarity of play-type initiation share vectors (types with cumulative share <1% pooled into 'OTHER'). The absent player's primary types are those with a pre-absence initiation share of at least 15% of his own total initiations. Gain is the change in the teammate's initiations per game in those primary types from the pre-absence to the absence window. Sensitivity checks: use Euclidean distance, and alternative gain measure (change in share of team initiations). Control for same-position indicator (based on common basketball-reference position) and change in total minutes played to isolate profile similarity from opportunity.",
    "falsification": "1. Shuffle the absent player's primary type labels to a random set of play types; expect the effect to disappear. 2. Run the same analysis on a set of placebo absences (random game stretches where the player actually played) to verify no spurious signal. 3. Split each absence spell into first half and second half; if the effect is driven by immediate coaching adjustments rather than stable profile similarity, it should weaken in the second half.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register a single primary test: cosine similarity and initiations-per-game gain. All other metrics and explorations are secondary and reported with Holm-Bonferroni corrected p-values. The primary metric for 'closest' is initiation profile similarity; the alternatives (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) are exploratory.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis of no association if the combined p-value across spells (via Stouffer's method from spell-level Spearman rho, or a permutation test of the average rank correlation) is below 0.05. Report the effect size (average rank correlation) and a 95% confidence interval. Conclude 'supported' only if the effect is positive and statistically significant after multiplicity correction.",
    "data_version": "NBA play-by-play and derived play-type possession records frozen as of 2026-11-01, using the project's inferrer and play-type classifier version active on that date."
  }
}}

